<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.15.2: Determining if Points on Boundary Lines Are Solutions to a System</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00649</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.15.2: Determining if Points on Boundary Lines Are Solutions to a System</md:title>
    <md:uuid>12605343-ef87-4221-a0ea-e7dd720ffd44</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>Here are the graphs of the inequalities in this system:</para>
      <!--BEGIN SYSTEM OF EQUATIONS -->
      <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}x\;\lt\;y\\y\geq\;-2x\;-\;6\end{array}\right.\)</para>
      <!--END SYSTEM OF EQUATIONS -->
      <!--BEGIN SYSTEM OF EQUATIONS-->
      <div>
        <div>
          <figure>
            <media alt="A graph of two intersecting inequalities on a coordinate plane.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_17_03_01.svg"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
      <para>Decide whether each point is a solution to the system. Be prepared to explain how you know.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>\((3,-5)\)</item>
      </list>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
  </problem>
        <solution>
          <div class="body"/>
          <div class="body">Compare your answer:</div>
          <div class="body">
            <para>No. \((3,-5)\) is in the shaded region containing the solutions to one of the inequalities but not in the region where the shading overlaps.</para>
          </div>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>\((0,5)\)</item>
      </list>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
  </problem>
        <solution>
          <div class="body"/>
          <div class="body">Compare your answer:</div>
          <div class="body">
            <para>Yes. \((0,5)\) is in the region where the solutions to the two inequalities overlap.</para>
          </div>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>\((-6,6)\)</item>
      </list>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
  </problem>
        <solution>
          <div class="body"/>
          <div class="body">Compare your answer:</div>
          <div class="body">
            <para>Yes. (\(-6,6)\) is on the solid boundary of \(y\geq-2x-6\) because \(0=-2(-3)−6\), and it is within the shaded region representing solutions to \(x&lt;y\).
     </para>
          </div>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>\((3,3)\)</item>
      </list>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
  </problem>
        <solution>
          <div class="body"/>
          <div class="body">Compare your answer:</div>
          <div class="body">
            <para>No. While \((3,3)\) is clearly a solution to \(y\geq-2x-6\), it is on the dashed boundary of \(x &lt; y\). The statement \(3&lt;3\) is not true.
     </para>
          </div>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>\((-2,-2)\)</item>
      </list>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
  </problem>
        <solution>
          <div class="body"/>
          <div class="body">Compare your answer:</div>
          <div class="body">
            <para>No, because \(-2&lt;-2\) is not true. Even though it is on the solid boundary of one, it is on the dashed boundary of the other.
     </para>
          </div>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <title> Video:</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a22e01b36d52e5c07062af988ec55db7cec27d65"/>
      </media>
